WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.608
Last week I put up a video introducing quantum computing,

00:00:02.608 --> 00:00:06.680
and in the final section we were stepping through something known as Grover's algorithm.

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:10.958
And based on the comments that I saw, I think there was a very common point of confusion

00:00:10.958 --> 00:00:14.560
that reveals I clearly could have done a better job explaining a core piece of it.

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:16.800
Right here I wanted to throw together a very quick

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:18.860
supplement in the hopes of adding a bit of clarity.

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:23.670
The premise was to have a function which is somehow triggered by a unique value

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:27.768
out of a big bag of options, and the puzzle is basically to figure out how do

00:00:27.768 --> 00:00:31.760
you find that unique value just by applying the function on various inputs.

00:00:32.259 --> 00:00:35.171
Now in a classical setting, it's not a very interesting question,

00:00:35.171 --> 00:00:38.934
the best you can do is guess and check, but what we walked through was a completely

00:00:38.934 --> 00:00:42.474
different approach that you can take that becomes possible in the setting of a

00:00:42.474 --> 00:00:43.280
quantum computer.

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:46.387
When we did this, there was a certain key step where,

00:00:46.387 --> 00:00:49.581
if I'm understanding the comments correctly, it looked to a lot of people

00:00:49.581 --> 00:00:52.731
like in order to apply this key step, you would have to already know the

00:00:52.731 --> 00:00:55.838
value that you're searching for, which would of course defeat the whole

00:00:55.838 --> 00:00:56.960
purpose of the algorithm.

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:00.935
More specifically, we had this very high dimensional vector space,

00:01:00.935 --> 00:01:05.697
and one of the axes in that space corresponded to the value that we're searching for,

00:01:05.697 --> 00:01:09.518
and this step of the algorithm looked like flipping along that axis,

00:01:09.518 --> 00:01:13.560
multiplying any component of a vector in that direction by negative one.

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:16.622
Now viewers were essentially asking, whoa whoa whoa,

00:01:16.622 --> 00:01:19.729
how could you know how to do that without already knowing which axis

00:01:19.729 --> 00:01:20.720
you're searching for?

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:24.494
I genuinely tried to forestall that objection, but I think I failed,

00:01:24.494 --> 00:01:27.897
so backing up, I think the whole discussion might be clearer if we

00:01:27.897 --> 00:01:31.300
focus on a very concrete example, something like solving a sudoku.

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:35.991
On your normal classical computer, it's not hard to write a function that checks

00:01:35.991 --> 00:01:40.360
whether a proposed solution follows all of the sudoku rules and solves the puzzle.

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:44.240
You know, it would check the rows, the columns, squares for duplicates, things like that.

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:48.971
If you have written this function, just because you know how to verify a solution,

00:01:48.971 --> 00:01:52.340
it's not at all obvious what the solution is in the first place.

00:01:52.340 --> 00:01:55.100
This is, after all, why a sudoku is a puzzle.

00:01:55.580 --> 00:01:57.700
The rules alone don't reveal the answer.

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:01.780
There are other situations where this is actually a much stronger assumption.

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:06.780
The function SHA-256, for example, is what's called a cryptographic hash function.

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:11.094
That basically means if you want to find what input gives you a particular output,

00:02:11.094 --> 00:02:14.211
it is strongly believed that you really can't gain much insight

00:02:14.211 --> 00:02:16.500
by looking at how the function is implemented.

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:20.365
If someone could reverse engineer it, they would be mining all of the

00:02:20.365 --> 00:02:23.730
bitcoin in the world and breaking numerous other cryptographic schemes.

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:26.795
But it's believed that the best thing you can do when you're

00:02:26.795 --> 00:02:29.050
searching for a particular output is guess and check.

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:34.056
So it's not that the key value is like hiding inside the function behind some curtain,

00:02:34.056 --> 00:02:38.090
it's more of a difficult-to-find emergent phenomenon of the function itself.

00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:42.487
Now the idea with Grover's algorithm is that if you have this sort of verifier

00:02:42.487 --> 00:02:46.078
function for some hard problem and you translate it into the language of

00:02:46.078 --> 00:02:49.767
quantum computing, there is a method for sifting out valid solutions which

00:02:49.767 --> 00:02:53.850
requires fewer steps than simple guessing and checking over all the possibilities.

00:02:54.470 --> 00:02:58.140
Now to be clear, it is not dramatically faster, it's only a quadratic speedup,

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:00.915
and given the overheads for making quantum computing work,

00:03:00.915 --> 00:03:02.750
this frankly has questionable utility.

00:03:03.110 --> 00:03:05.610
In fact, let's talk a little bit more about that at the end.

00:03:06.250 --> 00:03:10.001
First, to the clarification, if you have this Sudoku verifying function,

00:03:10.001 --> 00:03:12.970
it's not like you can just run it on a quantum computer.

00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:16.350
After all, quantum computers speak an entirely different language,

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:20.046
it's a totally different framework for computing that looks a lot more like vector

00:03:20.046 --> 00:03:20.670
manipulation.

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:25.078
The first step to port over this verification function into the

00:03:25.078 --> 00:03:28.363
new context is to imagine that we've kind of compiled your

00:03:28.363 --> 00:03:32.150
verifier into a bunch of logic gates, things like AND, OR, and NOT.

00:03:32.750 --> 00:03:37.026
So for any proposed Sudoku solution, you would represent it all in binary,

00:03:37.026 --> 00:03:40.781
all of those bits would be processed by your web of logic gates,

00:03:40.781 --> 00:03:44.306
and the output would be a 1 if it's a valid Sudoku solution,

00:03:44.306 --> 00:03:46.270
and a 0 for all the invalid ones.

00:03:46.850 --> 00:03:50.103
And again, being able to assemble these logic gates does not

00:03:50.103 --> 00:03:53.410
require knowing ahead of time which input solves the puzzle.

00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:56.790
The logic gates distill the rules of the game, but not the strategy.

00:03:57.950 --> 00:04:00.711
Now I'm assuming everyone has watched the main video,

00:04:00.711 --> 00:04:04.619
in particular the core section about the fundamentals of the state vector,

00:04:04.619 --> 00:04:08.214
but as a quick recap, the upshot is that you think of every possible

00:04:08.214 --> 00:04:12.590
bit string as a unit vector along a coordinate axis in some high dimensional space.

00:04:12.970 --> 00:04:15.263
In the language of linear algebra, you would call

00:04:15.263 --> 00:04:17.649
these the basis vectors of your coordinate system.

00:04:18.290 --> 00:04:22.826
For example, with a 2-qubit quantum computer, you would have 4 possible bit strings,

00:04:22.826 --> 00:04:26.930
and these would all look like basis directions in some 4-dimensional space.

00:04:27.670 --> 00:04:29.930
These state vectors get very big very fast.

00:04:30.290 --> 00:04:34.980
If you have a k-qubit quantum computer, that gives you 2 to the k possible bit strings,

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:38.538
and you think of each one of them as being a coordinate direction

00:04:38.538 --> 00:04:40.910
in some very high dimensional vector space.

00:04:41.550 --> 00:04:44.167
Now operations on a quantum computer don't spit out true

00:04:44.167 --> 00:04:46.690
or false the way you can see on a classical computer.

00:04:47.210 --> 00:04:50.201
Instead they take in a vector and spit out a new vector,

00:04:50.201 --> 00:04:52.230
both of which live in the same space.

00:04:52.790 --> 00:04:55.403
And like I said last video, you often think about them as

00:04:55.403 --> 00:04:57.970
somehow flipping or rotating the vectors in that space.

00:04:58.730 --> 00:05:00.170
Now here's the crux of the confusion.

00:05:00.510 --> 00:05:03.762
I mentioned how if you have this classical verifier function,

00:05:03.762 --> 00:05:06.906
something like a Sudoku checker that spits out a 1 or a 0,

00:05:06.906 --> 00:05:10.957
it is possible to translate it into an operation on a quantum computer that

00:05:10.957 --> 00:05:12.450
has the following behavior.

00:05:12.810 --> 00:05:16.603
If a bit string returns 1 for true up in the classical case,

00:05:16.603 --> 00:05:20.521
then down in the quantum case, the corresponding basis vector

00:05:20.521 --> 00:05:24.630
gets multiplied by negative 1, effectively flipping 180 degrees.

00:05:24.970 --> 00:05:28.722
And then if in the classical case a bit string outputs 0 for false,

00:05:28.722 --> 00:05:33.090
then in the quantum translation, the corresponding basis vector is unchanged.

00:05:33.670 --> 00:05:37.390
Now I can see three reasons that this step might have caused some confusion.

00:05:38.090 --> 00:05:40.430
First of all, I didn't explain how it actually works.

00:05:40.570 --> 00:05:41.870
I didn't step through the translation.

00:05:42.530 --> 00:05:46.037
Now my hope with that video was that it's just not too huge a leap to

00:05:46.037 --> 00:05:49.442
have you accept that in principle there exists this correspondence

00:05:49.442 --> 00:05:52.644
between returning true and false up in the classical world and

00:05:52.644 --> 00:05:56.710
multiplying by negative 1 or positive 1 down in the quantum world with vectors.

00:05:57.070 --> 00:05:58.030
Now maybe that is a leap.

00:05:58.210 --> 00:06:00.650
I could preview for you what that translation looks like.

00:06:00.650 --> 00:06:04.397
The relevant search term here is quantum compilation, but I'm going to be honest,

00:06:04.397 --> 00:06:08.099
I don't think it would add much clarity, in the same way that knowing the logic

00:06:08.099 --> 00:06:12.170
gates that implement addition don't really teach you much about how to add two numbers.

00:06:12.670 --> 00:06:16.690
Now it's not exactly like this, but loosely speaking, every time you see an AND gate,

00:06:16.690 --> 00:06:20.190
you translate it into a quantum operation that looks kind of like an AND.

00:06:20.490 --> 00:06:22.410
Every time you see a NOT gate, there's a quantum

00:06:22.410 --> 00:06:24.370
analogue that does something kind of like a NOT.

00:06:24.990 --> 00:06:29.721
I suspect the real cause of confusion originates not from a lack of low-level detail,

00:06:29.721 --> 00:06:32.170
but from how I had framed the entire setup.

00:06:32.510 --> 00:06:36.363
I opened that video by having you imagine that there was some mystery number that

00:06:36.363 --> 00:06:39.455
we're searching for, and as one commenter helpfully pointed out,

00:06:39.455 --> 00:06:43.070
this made it seem like the computer kind of knows the answer ahead of time,

00:06:43.070 --> 00:06:44.450
it's just hiding it from us.

00:06:44.690 --> 00:06:47.572
And this is almost certainly exacerbated by me briefly flashing

00:06:47.572 --> 00:06:50.179
an example function that just checks if the input is 12,

00:06:50.179 --> 00:06:53.290
and saying that we were going to treat the function as a black box.

00:06:53.630 --> 00:06:54.250
That's on me.

00:06:54.410 --> 00:06:56.010
That's a misleading way to open things.

00:06:56.010 --> 00:06:59.223
What I wanted to foreshadow is how, with Grover's algorithm,

00:06:59.223 --> 00:07:03.346
the only way you use the new quantum function is by trying it out on inputs,

00:07:03.346 --> 00:07:05.970
as opposed to maybe like reverse engineering it.

00:07:06.290 --> 00:07:08.750
So in that sense, it's treated as a black box.

00:07:09.170 --> 00:07:13.394
But to be clear, in order to translate the classical verifier into the quantum version,

00:07:13.394 --> 00:07:16.210
you absolutely need to get into the guts of the function.

00:07:16.530 --> 00:07:18.724
And if this is going to be a compelling example,

00:07:18.724 --> 00:07:22.381
it would be very silly if the only thing the function did was just check if the

00:07:22.381 --> 00:07:23.890
input equals some hidden number.

00:07:23.890 --> 00:07:27.162
The Sudoku example is much better, and a cryptographic

00:07:27.162 --> 00:07:29.950
hash like SHA-256 would be much better still.

00:07:30.290 --> 00:07:33.466
In these contexts, there is one value that will trigger the function,

00:07:33.466 --> 00:07:37.010
and we don't know what it is, but the computer also doesn't know what it is.

00:07:37.230 --> 00:07:40.130
It's not like the key value is just hiding in the source code.

00:07:40.390 --> 00:07:42.838
Whether we're up here in the classical setting,

00:07:42.838 --> 00:07:47.213
where triggering the function means returning true, or down in the quantum setting,

00:07:47.213 --> 00:07:50.598
where triggering the function means multiplying by negative one,

00:07:50.598 --> 00:07:54.296
which specific key input does this is a difficult to find and emergent

00:07:54.296 --> 00:07:55.910
property of those logic gates.

00:07:56.270 --> 00:07:58.330
It's not something that's baked in ahead of time.

00:07:58.730 --> 00:08:01.480
The other potential source of confusion, I suspect,

00:08:01.480 --> 00:08:04.930
is that I didn't appropriately emphasize the idea of linearity.

00:08:05.310 --> 00:08:09.596
In fact, this is a central enough feature of quantum computing and quantum mechanics that

00:08:09.596 --> 00:08:13.930
half of my reason for making this whole follow-up video is as an excuse to talk about it.

00:08:14.310 --> 00:08:17.184
So most vectors don't look like a pure basis direction,

00:08:17.184 --> 00:08:20.790
they look like some weighted sum of all the different basis vectors.

00:08:21.310 --> 00:08:24.033
One way you can represent this is with a column vector,

00:08:24.033 --> 00:08:28.490
where we think of each component as being associated with one of the possible bitstrings.

00:08:28.910 --> 00:08:33.023
The more common convention among physicists is to write general vectors as an

00:08:33.023 --> 00:08:37.510
explicit weighted sum of all the basis directions, each one represented with a ket.

00:08:37.970 --> 00:08:42.717
When the state vector for a computer looks like this, you say that it's in superposition,

00:08:42.717 --> 00:08:47.250
meaning it has some non-zero component associated with multiple distinct bitstrings.

00:08:47.810 --> 00:08:50.686
It's a lot like saying if someone is walking northeast,

00:08:50.686 --> 00:08:53.458
their velocity is a superposition of north and east,

00:08:53.458 --> 00:08:56.230
they're travelling both directions at the same time.

00:08:56.650 --> 00:08:59.780
A core idea from the last video is that you never actually see

00:08:59.780 --> 00:09:02.910
the coordinates of a state vector in superposition like this.

00:09:03.250 --> 00:09:08.002
When you read out from the computer, all you see is one of the bitstrings at random,

00:09:08.002 --> 00:09:12.133
and the probability of seeing it is equal to the square of the magnitude

00:09:12.133 --> 00:09:15.810
of the component of the state vector associated with that value.

00:09:15.810 --> 00:09:19.708
I'm saying magnitude here with the absolute value signs, because in general,

00:09:19.708 --> 00:09:22.836
these components can be complex numbers, but for simplicity,

00:09:22.836 --> 00:09:24.990
I'm only going to be showing real values.

00:09:25.790 --> 00:09:29.141
When I say that operations in quantum computers are linear,

00:09:29.141 --> 00:09:33.060
what I mean is that if you pass in one of these weighted sums of the

00:09:33.060 --> 00:09:36.524
different basis directions, that is to say, a superposition,

00:09:36.524 --> 00:09:39.364
then the output looks like the same weighted sum,

00:09:39.364 --> 00:09:42.090
but of the transformed versions of each vector.

00:09:42.570 --> 00:09:44.130
So here's a very small example.

00:09:44.490 --> 00:09:47.670
On a single qubit, there's an operation that we call a z-gate.

00:09:48.170 --> 00:09:51.346
What it does is it leaves the 0 direction unchanged,

00:09:51.346 --> 00:09:54.950
but it multiplies that vertical 1 direction by negative 1.

00:09:55.490 --> 00:09:58.572
These are only two out of the infinitely many possible state vectors,

00:09:58.572 --> 00:10:00.090
but they're all you need to know.

00:10:00.570 --> 00:10:04.015
If you pass in a superposition of those two, something that has a

00:10:04.015 --> 00:10:07.620
little bit of 0 plus a little bit of 1, what you do is look at what

00:10:07.620 --> 00:10:11.330
the z-gate does to each part separately, and then add those together.

00:10:11.330 --> 00:10:12.890
Again, with the same components.

00:10:13.410 --> 00:10:17.290
In this case, that means flipping the sign associated with the 1 component.

00:10:17.730 --> 00:10:20.851
Geometrically, when you draw this vector in a 2D space,

00:10:20.851 --> 00:10:24.370
the action of a z-gate looks like flipping around the x-axis.

00:10:24.890 --> 00:10:28.062
The z-gate is simple enough that just by looking at the definition,

00:10:28.062 --> 00:10:30.430
you can clearly see which direction gets flipped.

00:10:30.950 --> 00:10:33.821
But keep in mind, for more complicated functions,

00:10:33.821 --> 00:10:37.570
the definition alone might not so easily reveal how it behaves.

00:10:38.190 --> 00:10:43.201
Take a look back at the Sudoku verification function and its translation onto a quantum

00:10:43.201 --> 00:10:48.211
computer, and then say that the state of your computer is not one of those clean basis

00:10:48.211 --> 00:10:51.782
directions, but it is a combination of all the basis vectors,

00:10:51.782 --> 00:10:56.908
a superposition of every possible bit string in this context representing every possible

00:10:56.908 --> 00:10:58.290
solution to the Sudoku.

00:10:58.890 --> 00:11:02.116
To figure out what our verifier does to this new vector,

00:11:02.116 --> 00:11:05.918
what you do is look at what it would do to each basis separately,

00:11:05.918 --> 00:11:09.950
and then the output is going to be the same scaled sum of the result.

00:11:10.530 --> 00:11:14.155
In this case, most parts of that sum stay unchanged, but one of them,

00:11:14.155 --> 00:11:18.358
the one that's associated with the key input that represents a Sudoku solution,

00:11:18.358 --> 00:11:20.670
that part is going to have it sign flipped.

00:11:21.250 --> 00:11:25.411
When you do this, it's very tempting to look at it and say that the function is acting

00:11:25.411 --> 00:11:29.330
on every possible basis vector at once in parallel, and then adding the results.

00:11:29.710 --> 00:11:32.346
Now that might be true, but I invite you to reflect on

00:11:32.346 --> 00:11:35.030
whether that's necessarily a fair way to summarize it.

00:11:35.290 --> 00:11:37.995
As an analogy, if a hiker is walking northeast,

00:11:37.995 --> 00:11:42.370
and you tell him to rotate 90 degrees, that rotation is a linear operation.

00:11:42.730 --> 00:11:47.616
In other words, the final direction is the same as what you would get by rotating the

00:11:47.616 --> 00:11:52.790
north vector 90 degrees, rotating the east vector 90 degrees, and adding the two results.

00:11:53.210 --> 00:11:55.623
But that doesn't mean that you have to perform two

00:11:55.623 --> 00:11:58.470
separate rotations in parallel in order to move the hiker.

00:11:58.770 --> 00:12:01.506
The linearity is a property of the transformation,

00:12:01.506 --> 00:12:04.790
it's not necessarily a set of instructions on how to do it.

00:12:05.310 --> 00:12:06.530
It's very similar over here.

00:12:07.010 --> 00:12:10.545
The effect of the quantum translation for our verifier looks like

00:12:10.545 --> 00:12:14.351
adding together what its effect would be on all of the basis vectors,

00:12:14.351 --> 00:12:17.995
but I will leave it to your interpretation whether this means that

00:12:17.995 --> 00:12:21.910
it is necessarily acting on all 2 to the k possible bitstrings at once.

00:12:22.370 --> 00:12:25.241
Here, I've been writing the vector the physicist way,

00:12:25.241 --> 00:12:27.840
but if I write it with a column vector instead,

00:12:27.840 --> 00:12:31.631
what this operation looks like is taking one component of that vector

00:12:31.631 --> 00:12:35.260
and multiplying it by negative 1, specifically whichever component

00:12:35.260 --> 00:12:37.210
corresponds to the sudoku solution.

00:12:38.130 --> 00:12:40.475
So stepping back, hopefully this helps clear up

00:12:40.475 --> 00:12:43.070
some of the confusion there was in that last video.

00:12:43.490 --> 00:12:47.937
The starting point of Grover's algorithm is to assume that you're given a function

00:12:47.937 --> 00:12:52.330
that flips one component of a vector like this, though you don't know which one.

00:12:52.630 --> 00:12:56.640
The puzzle is to somehow figure out which direction is getting flipped,

00:12:56.640 --> 00:13:01.666
where all you're allowed to do is apply this function to some well-chosen set of inputs,

00:13:01.666 --> 00:13:05.676
and in Grover's case, it involves interleaving it with a certain other

00:13:05.676 --> 00:13:08.330
operation in the toolkit of quantum computing.

00:13:08.750 --> 00:13:11.370
I get it, that is a really weird place to start from.

00:13:11.570 --> 00:13:14.370
And it doesn't help that this is a famously confusing topic.

00:13:14.570 --> 00:13:17.932
If you find it weird to work with a state vector whose components

00:13:17.932 --> 00:13:21.139
you never actually observe and which instead acts like a kind

00:13:21.139 --> 00:13:24.450
of square root of a probability distribution, you're not alone.

00:13:24.450 --> 00:13:26.490
Everyone finds that very weird.

00:13:26.990 --> 00:13:29.967
At this point I genuinely can't tell if I'm overexplaining things

00:13:29.967 --> 00:13:32.944
or still under-explaining them, but there is one final aspect of

00:13:32.944 --> 00:13:36.150
the explanation from last time that may have added to this confusion.

00:13:36.630 --> 00:13:40.284
When we visualized the algorithm, we chose to view everything on a

00:13:40.284 --> 00:13:43.993
certain two-dimensional slice of the enormous n-dimensional vector

00:13:43.993 --> 00:13:48.034
space where all these state vectors live, and this slice, by definition,

00:13:48.034 --> 00:13:52.130
included the axis associated with that mystery value we're searching for.

00:13:52.130 --> 00:13:55.706
Now, in case that left anyone with the impression that part of the

00:13:55.706 --> 00:13:59.390
algorithm was to choose that slice, let me be very clear, it's not.

00:13:59.690 --> 00:14:01.990
The algorithm is just doing what it's going to do.

00:14:02.210 --> 00:14:04.610
It interleaves two operations that go back and forth.

00:14:04.890 --> 00:14:08.230
It doesn't have a care in the world how you and I choose to visualize it.

00:14:08.470 --> 00:14:12.718
The fact that the state vector stays confined to this particular plane,

00:14:12.718 --> 00:14:15.770
that's a happy emergent property of the algorithm.

00:14:16.090 --> 00:14:19.610
It is in no way part of the instruction set that we're giving to the computer.

00:14:20.350 --> 00:14:23.979
One very final thing that I do think deserves some added reflection is how

00:14:23.979 --> 00:14:28.050
Grover's algorithm, while very thought-provoking, is maybe just not really useful.

00:14:28.550 --> 00:14:29.570
Take the Sudoku example.

00:14:29.910 --> 00:14:33.577
The number of possible solutions will depend on how many of those 81

00:14:33.577 --> 00:14:37.730
squares start off blank, but let's call it something like 9 to the power 60.

00:14:38.190 --> 00:14:43.034
If you tried to use your classical verifier function to find a solution by brute force,

00:14:43.034 --> 00:14:45.930
there are way, way too many possibilities to check.

00:14:45.930 --> 00:14:48.933
But even if you had a fully functioning quantum computer,

00:14:48.933 --> 00:14:52.884
sitting on your desk right now with ample qubits and no issues maintaining

00:14:52.884 --> 00:14:55.518
coherence and all that, using Grover's algorithm,

00:14:55.518 --> 00:14:59.733
this would still take around 9 to the 30 steps, which is a much smaller number,

00:14:59.733 --> 00:15:01.050
but it's still enormous.

00:15:01.530 --> 00:15:05.470
In this case, there's obviously many smarter ways to solve a Sudoku than

00:15:05.470 --> 00:15:08.862
a brute force search, but if you take something like SHA-256,

00:15:08.862 --> 00:15:13.130
inverting it by brute force takes 2 to the 256 steps on a classical computer.

00:15:13.510 --> 00:15:16.406
Using Grover's algorithm, that becomes 2 to the 128,

00:15:16.406 --> 00:15:19.970
at least up to that pi-fourths constant that we saw last video.

00:15:20.450 --> 00:15:24.296
So even in some sci-fi future where quantum computers are as far along as

00:15:24.296 --> 00:15:28.510
today's classical computers, that is still an infeasibly large number of steps.

00:15:29.050 --> 00:15:31.075
The way some people write about quantum computing,

00:15:31.075 --> 00:15:32.978
it makes it sound like the moment they arrive,

00:15:32.978 --> 00:15:35.650
everything is going to change and all of cryptography will break.

00:15:36.110 --> 00:15:40.067
And it's true that there are specific problems that have exponential speedups,

00:15:40.067 --> 00:15:43.567
and especially with RSA, some of those are relevant to cryptography,

00:15:43.567 --> 00:15:45.190
but that's not true in general.

00:15:45.450 --> 00:15:49.490
This quadratic speedup is much more representative of what you get for most problems.

00:15:49.730 --> 00:15:52.032
It's really cool, and the math is just beautiful,

00:15:52.032 --> 00:15:55.273
and there continues to be lots of interesting research in the field,

00:15:55.273 --> 00:15:58.655
but one of my hopes with this whole project is that you now have enough

00:15:58.655 --> 00:16:01.803
background to maybe see through some of the hyperbole that certain

00:16:01.803 --> 00:16:02.930
outlets are so fond of.

00:16:22.730 --> 00:16:22.810
Thank you.

